MID_SE_Tia_Bon_C: <EDH>
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_C_001: Heeheehee. I borrowed a lot. I wonder how quickly I\ncan read them and how soon I can get started.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_C_002: You are Tialla, yes? That is a sizable parcel,\nyoung maiden.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_C_003: Oh! Bonaventure. Yes, I have to lug around all these\nbooks I got from the temple.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_C_004: Indeed? All these texts came from the temple?!
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_C_005: Let me help you carry them to their destination.\nI would hear the whole story from you.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_C_006: And to be clear, you intend to read all of these?
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_C_007: Yes! I've loved books ever since I was little. Reading\nalways makes me feel calm.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_C_008: I want to read all day every day. Eating, bathing,\nwalking. I'd read in my sleep, if I could.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_C_009: Not a sentiment one would hear in Saramis,\nI must say.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_C_010: Because written words are prohibited, right?
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_C_011: Is it...rude, for me to speak about books to you?
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_C_012: Perish the thought, young maiden.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_C_013: But you said you borrowed these from the temple.\nHow did you secure permission?
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_C_014: I begged someone I know. I said I would do anything\nto read the rare books in the temple collection.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_C_015: My grandfather was a tactician, and I thought he had\na lot of books. But they have titles I've never seen.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_C_016: Hm. I suppose the temples would keep their own\nrepositories of valuable texts.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_C_017: I have to admit, I've got mixed feelings about their\napproach to books, though.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_C_018: Temples decide which books they don't agree with.\nAnd then they ban them...or burn them.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_C_019: But I guess I shouldn't be too harsh with them if\nthey're letting me borrow from their collection.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_C_020: Ha! Tialla, your mixed feelings are the sign of a\nrefined mind.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_C_021: A tactician like you ought to know, so I'm sure\nyou're right.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_C_022: But I do owe the temples some gratitude, at least.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_C_023: Oh! The sun's already setting.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_C_024: I have to run, Bonaventure! I'm sorry! Let's\ntalk again!
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_C_025: I hope I can finish all these before they're due back\nto the temples.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_C_026: Such a desire to learn, at such a young age...\nA maiden with an open mind can go far.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_C_027: I thought her ordinary enough, but she has already\nfar surpassed my expectations.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_B: <EDH>
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_B_001: Very interesting. So your people perceive Yu Phas in\nmuch the same way as the others.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_B_002: Yes. We see her as a fearsome goddess of death.\nBut I understand she's worshipped in Saramis.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_B_003: Nothing that lives can escape death.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_B_004: Yu Phas's duty is to guide the souls of the departed.\nIn that, you understand her correctly.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_B_005: But there is no question of good or evil in it. It is\nsimply the task she performs.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_B_006: No, the reason we revere Yu Phas is for saving our\nancestors from Dagda the Great.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_B_007: The People of the White Egg, right? The ones who\nlater founded Damascar.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_B_008: That is correct. The children Yu Phas saved later\nmatured. Their children crowned the first king.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_B_009: Alas, that land too was ravaged by the\nDivine Emperor.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_B_010: The Cleansing of Damascar. That's how the royal\ncapital Ahm Lu became a dead city.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_B_011: The royals who survived Damascar were the\nancestors of the Savangren dynasty of Saramis.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_B_012: We harbor an undying resentment of the emperor\nand all the priests who carried out his vile bidding.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_B_013: About that—are we sure the priests really did\nfollow him?
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_B_014: Here's a collection of notes from priests who\nwitnessed the destruction of Damascar.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_B_015: What?! Such records have survived?!
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_B_016: Give it to—I mean, read it to me. The written word\nwould do me no good, of course.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_B_017: Yes, I was about to do just that. Here's an example\nfrom one of the priests.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_B_018: "Great Divine Emperor, whence the change of heart?\nWhy carry out the annihilation of Damascar?
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_B_019: "Did you not acknowledge the blessed existence of\nthat kingdom?"
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_B_020: I never knew so many priests were sympathetic\ntoward Damascar...even protesting the emperor.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_B_021: In most of my history books, Damascar was a fierce\nnation that followed a dark and sinister goddess.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_B_022: But that's not true. The people fought for the honor\nof Yu Phas. They fought for dignity.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_B_023: Just so. And I was mistaken as well. I thought the\npriests to be gleeful followers of the Divine Emperor.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_B_024: Blinded by my hate for those who wronged my\nancestors, I took for granted a warped tale. Shameful.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_B_025: Bonaventure...
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_B_026: The sentiments of a priest from the distant past now\noccupy my mind. Is this the power of writing?
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_B_027: What you have related to me is fascinating. I thank\nyou for it.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_B_028: When you say that, it gives me the confidence to\nkeep learning more and more.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_B_029: Every time we talk, I take away something new.\nWe have to do this again.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_B_030: Tialla's keen eye and erudition are worth paying\nclose attention to, I would wager.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_B_031: Her mind may one day exceed my own. Which may\npresent an opportunity...
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A: <EDH>
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A_001: And so we see in this a perfect example of a\nlarge-scale combat engagement. Do you follow?
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A_002: Yes! And Arago's war chronicles tell of a time when\nthey exploited this tactic, right?
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A_003: The second king, Marquel, left deliberate gaps in the\nbattle formation to mislead the rebel army.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A_004: You are quick to grasp the lesson. I find our time\ntogether most exhilarating.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A_005: Heehee. The pleasure's all mine!
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A_006: Bonaventure, I don't know how you do it, but you\nout-grandpa my grandpa!
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A_007: And what do you mean by that? Certainly, I am no\nlonger in the prime of youth, but come now.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A_008: No, no! What I mean is, Grandpa was a brilliant\ntactician. Although...he scares me sometimes.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A_009: But you... You'd make a wonderful grandpa.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A_010: Hoho. Who could find insult in such sweetness?
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A_011: Though I have a solemn request to make of you.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A_012: Me? What can I do for you?
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A_013: I wish to invite you to my Saramis, my homeland.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A_014: Will you not come with me? I beg of you!
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A_015: What? But you're more than enough tactician for\nany kingdom.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A_016: You are an expert in that field, it is true. But the task\nI had in mind was to compile our history.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A_017: Tialla, your talents would make for an exceptional\nhistorian.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A_018: You are well-read in subjects across eras and regions.\nAnd discerning enough to spot small discrepancies.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A_019: You can even weave together accounts from multiple\ntexts into a single narrative.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A_020: I know of no other sage possessed of all these\nqualities at once.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A_021: Most importantly of all, you are highly literate.\nMore so than even your country's scribes.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A_022: But, well, my dream is to work for a temple someday.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A_023: Then...if you cannot serve in an official role, perhaps\nyou can visit as a guest of honor from the temples.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A_024: Your task would be to record, in secret, the history of\nSaramis and the storehouse of my mind. Please.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A_025: Why are you pushing this?
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A_026: As you have noted, I am old. And I am derelict in\nmy duty to my queen.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A_027: She charged me with remembrance of all our nation's\nknowledge—in a land where writing is forbidden.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A_028: If I die, all of it dies within me. And so, before\nthat happens...
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A_029: You want your memories put down in writing, to\nguide future generations. That's what it is, isn't it?
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A_030: Just so. You are wise indeed.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A_031: I see. I don't know that I'm the best candidate\nfor this.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A_032: But I'd be honored to help record the history\nof Saramis.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A_033: It gladdens my heart to hear that. I did not realize\nthe breath that I had been holding.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A_034: Oh...
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A_035: Do you see what old age has done to me already?\nI am no longer master of my emotions.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A_036: Don't talk like that, Bonaventure.
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A_037: Or should I say, Grandpa Bonaventure?
MID_SE_Tia_Bon_A_038: Oho! I should be so honored.